      "supplier_excerpts": [
        {
          "supplier": "def-complex-differentiability-holomorphic-and-entire",
          "carrier": "items/def-complex-differentiability-holomorphic-and-entire.md",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb C$ be open, let $a\\in U$, and let $f:U\\to\\mathbb C$. The function $f$ is **complex differentiable at $a$** if the limit\n\n$$ \\lim_{\\substack{h\\to0\\\\ h\\ne0,\\ a+h\\in U}}\\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h} $$\n\nexists in the metric of [[def-complex-metric-convergence-and-continuity]]. Its value is the **complex derivative** of $f$ at $a$, denoted $f'(a)$. The increments are nonzero and remain in the domain; because $U$ is open, all sufficiently small increments are allowed.\n\nThe function $f$ is **holomorphic on $U$** when it is complex differentiable at every point of $U$. A function holomorphic on all of $\\mathbb C$ is **entire**. The word analytic is reserved for the local power-series notion.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Use only under the exact hypotheses and conventions in the quoted section; the consumer states the specific use in its Facts & Assumptions.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "The current Definition section was inspected; this kind has no Proof section.",
          "consumer_use": "Direct dependency declared by def-nrqmx-resonance-matrix-element-poles; relevant local fact labels: "
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-resolvent-and-spectrum-of-a-closed-unbounded-operator",
          "carrier": "items/def-resolvent-and-spectrum-of-a-closed-unbounded-operator.md",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Let $H$ be a complex Hilbert space and $T$ a linear operator on $H$ with domain $D(T)$. Write $(z-T)x=zx-Tx$ for $x\\in D(T)$. The **resolvent set**\nof $T$ is\n$$\\rho(T):=\\{z\\in\\mathbb C:\\ z-T:D(T)\\to H\\text{ is bijective and }(z-T)^{-1}\\in\\mathcal B(H)\\},$$\nwhere $\\mathcal B(H)$ is the space of bounded everywhere defined operators on\n$H$ ([[def-bounded-linear-operator]]); the **spectrum** is\n$\\sigma(T):=\\mathbb C\\setminus\\rho(T)$. For $z\\in\\rho(T)$ the bounded operator\n$$R_T(z):=(z-T)^{-1}\\in\\mathcal B(H)$$\nis the **resolvent** of $T$ at $z$. This is the library convention used\nthroughout the page, matching the bounded resolvent of\n[[def-spectrum-and-resolvent-of-a-bounded-operator]]: the shift is $zI-T$, with coefficient $-1$ on $T$.\n\n**The resolvent determines $T$ back.** If $z\\in\\rho(T)$ and $R:=R_T(z)$, then\n$D(T)=\\operatorname{ran}R$ and $TRy=zRy-y$ for $y\\in H$; equivalently\n$T=zI-R^{-1}$ with $D(T)=\\operatorname{ran}R$, and $R(z-T)x=x$ for\n$x\\in D(T)$, $(z-T)Ry=y$ for $y\\in H$.\n\n**Nonempty resolvent set forces closedness, without the closed graph\ntheorem.** Assume $\\rho(T)\\ne\\varnothing$ and fix $z\\in\\rho(T)$ with\n$R:=R_T(z)$. Choose a bound $C\\ge0$ such that $\\|Rv\\|\\le C\\|v\\|$.\nThe map $F(v,w)=w-Rv$ is continuous, since\n$\\|F(v,w)-F(v',w')\\|\\le\\|w-w'\\|+C\\|v-v'\\|$.\nIts zero set $\\Gamma(R)=F^{-1}(\\{0\\})$ is closed. Because\n$D(T)=\\operatorname{ran}R$ and $(z-T)Rv=v$ for every $v\\in H$, the graph of $T$ is\n$$\\Gamma(T)=\\{(x,Tx):x\\in D(T)\\}=\\{(Rv,zRv-v):v\\in H\\}=\\Phi(\\Gamma(R)),$$\nwhere $\\Phi(v,w):=(w,zw-v)$ is a continuous linear bijection of $H\\oplus H$ with\ncontinuous inverse $\\Psi(u,v)=(zu-v,u)$, since $\\Phi(\\Psi(u,v))=(u,z u-(zu-v))=(u,v)$\nand $\\Psi(\\Phi(v,w))=\\Psi(w,zw-v)=(v,w)$. A homeomorphism carries closed sets\nto closed sets, so $\\Gamma(T)$ is closed and $T$ is closed.\nNo closed graph theorem and hence no choice principle is used here.\n\nFor the zero operator on a nonzero $H$, $\\rho(0)=\\mathbb C\\setminus\\{0\\}$: the nonzero shifts have inverse $z^{-1}I$, whereas the zero shift is not bijective. If $H=\\{0\\}$, every shift is the unique bijection of the zero space, so $\\rho(T)=\\mathbb C$ and $\\sigma(T)=\\varnothing$.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Use only under the exact hypotheses and conventions in the quoted section; the consumer states the specific use in its Facts & Assumptions.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "The current Definition section was inspected; this kind has no Proof section.",
          "consumer_use": "Direct dependency declared by def-nrqmx-resonance-matrix-element-poles; relevant local fact labels: "
        },
        {
