          "source": "thm-nrqm-composites-real-multipliers-and-free-bounded-potential-operators",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice and the Axiom of Choice ([[def-countable-choice]], [[def-axiom-of-choice]]), let $d\\ge1$ and work on the complex Hilbert space $H=L^2(\\mathbb R^d;\\mathbb C)$, whose completeness follows from [[thm-riesz-fischer-completeness-of-l-p]] and whose Hilbert-space structure is defined in [[def-hilbert-space]].\n\n(a) For a real measurable finite-valued function $a:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\mathbb R$ define $M_af:=af$ on $D(M_a):=\\{f\\in H: af\\in H\\}$. Then $M_a$ is a densely defined self-adjoint operator on $H$ ([[def-adjoint-of-a-densely-defined-unbounded-operator]], [[def-symmetric-self-adjoint-and-essentially-self-adjoint]]).\n\n(b) Let $c>0$, $b\\in\\mathbb R$, and let $\\mathcal F_2$ be the unitary Plancherel transform with the convention $\\widehat f(\\xi)=\\int f(x)e^{-2\\pi ix\\cdot\\xi}dx$ on $L^1$ ([[def-fourier-transform-on-l-one-of-rn]], [[thm-plancherel]]). Define\n\n$$H_0:=\\mathcal F_2^*\\,M_{c|\\xi|^2+b}\\,\\mathcal F_2,\\qquad D(H_0):=\\{f\\in H:|\\xi|^2\\mathcal F_2f\\in H\\}.$$\n\nThen $H_0$ is self-adjoint, $D(H_0)$ coincides with the Sobolev space $W^{2,2}(\\mathbb R^d;\\mathbb C)$ as a set (with equivalent norms, [[def-sobolev-space-wkp-and-its-norm]], [[thm-fourier-characterisation-of-integer-order-hilbert-sobolev-spaces]]), $H_0\\ge b$ in the quadratic-form sense, and for every Schwartz function $f\\in\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^d)$, $H_0f=-(c/4\\pi^2)\\Delta f+bf$.\n\n(c) If $V:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\mathbb R$ is measurable with $\\|V\\|_\\infty<\\infty$ and $H:=H_0+M_V$ with domain $D(H_0)$, then $H$ is self-adjoint on $D(H_0)$, is bounded below, and the assignment $t\\mapsto e^{itH}$ is a strongly continuous one-parameter unitary group whose generator is $iH$: the limit $\\lim_{t\\to0}\\frac1t(e^{itH}\\psi-\\psi)$ exists exactly for $\\psi\\in D(H)$ and equals $iH\\psi$ ([[lem-self-adjoint-operator-generates-a-strongly-continuous-unitary-group]]).\n\n(d) *Scope*: the argument uses only boundedness and measurability of $V$; it proves nothing for $V\\propto-1/|x|$, $V\\propto|x|^2$ or the delta interaction, does not identify $H_0$ with any measured free-particle Hamiltonian, and asserts no physical interpretation.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-gaussian-integral",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "$$\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}e^{-x^2}\\,dx=\\sqrt\\pi.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-polar-coordinates-formula-for-lebesgue-measure",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $n \\ge 1$,\nlet $S^{n-1} \\subseteq \\mathbb R^n$ be the unit sphere, and let $\\sigma$ be the\nset function of [[def-polar-surface-measure-on-the-unit-sphere]]. Then $\\sigma$\nis a finite Borel measure on $S^{n-1}$, and for every Borel measurable\n$f : \\mathbb R^n \\to [0,\\infty]$,\n$$\\int_{\\mathbb R^n} f(x)\\,d\\lambda_n(x) = \\int_0^\\infty \\int_{S^{n-1}} f(r\\omega) r^{n-1}\\,d\\sigma(\\omega)\\,dr.$$\nMoreover, $\\sigma$ is the unique Borel measure on $S^{n-1}$ with this\nproperty.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces, and let $f : X \\times Y \\to [0,\\infty]$ be product-measurable. Then\n$x \\mapsto \\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu$ and $y \\mapsto \\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu$ are measurable,\nand\n\n$$\\int_{X \\times Y} f\\,d(\\mu \\times \\nu) = \\int_X \\left(\\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu\\right)d\\mu = \\int_Y \\left(\\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu\\right)d\\nu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-dominated-convergence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be an arbitrary measure space. All functions below have domain $X$. Let $f$ and $(f_n)$ be measurable complex-valued functions such that\n$f_n\\to f$ almost everywhere and $|f_n|\\le g$ almost everywhere for a single\nnonnegative measurable function $g$ with $\\int g\\,d\\mu<+\\infty$. Then\n$f\\in L^1(\\mu)$,\n$$\\int|f_n-f|\\,d\\mu\\longrightarrow0,$$\nand hence\n$$\\int f_n\\,d\\mu\\longrightarrow\\int f\\,d\\mu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-identity-theorem-holomorphic-functions",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "If two holomorphic functions on a complex domain agree on a set with an accumulation point in the domain, then they agree everywhere on the domain.\n\nPrecisely, let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb C$ be a complex domain (A complex domain is a nonempty connected open subset of $\\mathbb C$), let $f,g:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb C$ be holomorphic, and suppose that some $a\\in\\Omega$ is an accumulation point of $\\{z\\in\\Omega:f(z)=g(z)\\}$. Then $f=g$ on $\\Omega$. The requirement $a\\in\\Omega$ is essential.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "By [F1], $H_0$ is self-adjoint and nonnegative. The Gaussian integral in [F2], applied in each coordinate and combined with Tonelli [F4], gives $\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}e^{-|p|^2}\\,dp=\\pi^{3/2}$, so $\\|g\\|=1$. Polar coordinates [F3] and the resolvent multiplier therefore give\n$m(z)=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt\\pi}\\int_0^\\infty\\frac{r^2e^{-r^2}}{r^2-z}\\,dr$\nfor $\\operatorname{Im}z>0$. The denominator is nonzero on the integration ray, and the integral is locally uniformly dominated on the upper half-plane, so it defines the stated resolvent matrix element.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "For $\\operatorname{Re}a>0$, put $I(a)=\\int_0^\\infty e^{-r^2}(r^2+a^2)^{-1}\\,dr$. When $a>0$ is real, the Laplace representation of $(r^2+a^2)^{-1}$ and Tonelli [F4] give $I(a)=\\int_0^\\infty e^{-a^2s}\\int_0^\\infty e^{-(1+s)r^2}\\,dr\\,ds=\\frac{\\sqrt\\pi}{2}\\int_0^\\infty\\frac{e^{-a^2s}}{\\sqrt{1+s}}\\,ds$ by [F2]. The substitutions $u=\\sqrt{1+s}$ and $v=au$ then give $I(a)=\\sqrt\\pi e^{a^2}\\int_1^\\infty e^{-a^2u^2}\\,du=\\frac{\\sqrt\\pi e^{a^2}}a\\int_a^\\infty e^{-v^2}\\,dv=\\frac{\\pi}{2a}e^{a^2}\\mathcal E(a)$, using the horizontal-ray definition of $\\mathcal E$ for real $a>0$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The function $I$ is holomorphic on $\\operatorname{Re}a>0$: on a compact subset of that half-plane, the denominator $r^2+a^2$ is uniformly bounded away from zero for bounded $r$, while $|r^2+a^2|\\ge r^2-|a|^2\\ge3r^2/4$ for $r\\ge2\\max|a|$. These estimates dominate the integrand and its $a$-derivative by an integrable Gaussian times a polynomial, so [F5] justifies differentiation under the integral. Writing $\\mathcal E(w)=\\frac2{\\sqrt\\pi}\\int_0^\\infty e^{-(w+t)^2}\\,dt$, the integrand and its $w$-derivative on each compact set of $w$ are bounded by $C(1+t)e^{-t^2/4}$ after enlarging $C$, so [F5] also proves that $\\mathcal E$ is entire. Thus both sides of the identity in step 1.2 are holomorphic on the connected half-plane $\\operatorname{Re}a>0$; since they agree for every positive real $a$, [F6] proves the identity throughout that half-plane.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.2",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "For $\\operatorname{Im}z>0$, choose the square root $a=\\sqrt{-z}$ with $\\operatorname{Re}a>0$. Since $r^2/(r^2-z)=1+z/(r^2-z)$, $m(z)=2+\\frac{4z}{\\sqrt\\pi}I(a)$; substituting the identity from step 2.1 and $z=-a^2$ gives $m(z)=2-2\\sqrt\\pi\\,a e^{-z}\\mathcal E(a)$.\nOn $D\\cap\\{\\operatorname{Im}z>0\\}$, the root $a$ equals $-ik(z)$: both square to $-z$, and $\\operatorname{Re}(-ik(z))>0$. Substitution gives $m(z)=2+2i\\sqrt\\pi\\,k(z)e^{-z}\\mathcal E(-ik(z))$ there. The right hand side is holomorphic throughout $D$, so it is the required local continuation through $E_0$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.1",
            "F2",
            "F5",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The model fixes the nonempty radial integration ray r in [0,infinity); there is no empty index set or family."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The center E0 is strictly positive and the disk is required to avoid zero, so the branch endpoint is excluded. See the Statement."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The form factor is one normalized Gaussian vector; its norm is computed in step 1.1. See the Statement."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The square-root branch is uniquely fixed by k(E0)=sqrt(E0)>0; the continuation is local on a disk avoiding zero. See the Statement."
        },
        {
