        {
          "supplier": "thm-spectral-theorem-for-unbounded-self-adjoint-operators",
          "carrier": "items/thm-spectral-theorem-for-unbounded-self-adjoint-operators.md",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $T$ be a self-adjoint operator on the nonzero\ncomplex Hilbert space $H$. Then there is a unique regular projection valued\nmeasure $E$ on the Borel $\\sigma$-algebra of $\\mathbb R$ such that\n$$\nD(T)=\\Bigl\\{x\\in H:\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\lambda^2\\,dE_x(\\lambda)<\\infty\\Bigr\\},\\qquad Tx=\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\lambda\\,dE(\\lambda)x\\quad(x\\in D(T)).\n$$\nConversely, if $E$ is a regular projection valued measure on $\\mathbb R$, then\nthe operator $\\int\\lambda\\,dE$ with that domain is self-adjoint and its\nspectral projection valued measure is $E$ again.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Use only under the exact hypotheses and conventions in the quoted section; the consumer states the specific use in its Facts & Assumptions.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published carrier; complete current Proof section inspected for the actual hypotheses and argument. This is not an independent approval claim.",
          "consumer_use": "Direct dependency declared by thm-nrqmx-rank-one-resonance-continuation; relevant local fact labels: F4"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-nrqm-composites-real-multipliers-and-free-bounded-potential-operators",
          "carrier": "items/thm-nrqm-composites-real-multipliers-and-free-bounded-potential-operators.md",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice and the Axiom of Choice ([[def-countable-choice]], [[def-axiom-of-choice]]), let $d\\ge1$ and work on the complex Hilbert space $H=L^2(\\mathbb R^d;\\mathbb C)$, whose completeness follows from [[thm-riesz-fischer-completeness-of-l-p]] and whose Hilbert-space structure is defined in [[def-hilbert-space]].\n\n(a) For a real measurable finite-valued function $a:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\mathbb R$ define $M_af:=af$ on $D(M_a):=\\{f\\in H: af\\in H\\}$. Then $M_a$ is a densely defined self-adjoint operator on $H$ ([[def-adjoint-of-a-densely-defined-unbounded-operator]], [[def-symmetric-self-adjoint-and-essentially-self-adjoint]]).\n\n(b) Let $c>0$, $b\\in\\mathbb R$, and let $\\mathcal F_2$ be the unitary Plancherel transform with the convention $\\widehat f(\\xi)=\\int f(x)e^{-2\\pi ix\\cdot\\xi}dx$ on $L^1$ ([[def-fourier-transform-on-l-one-of-rn]], [[thm-plancherel]]). Define\n\n$$H_0:=\\mathcal F_2^*\\,M_{c|\\xi|^2+b}\\,\\mathcal F_2,\\qquad D(H_0):=\\{f\\in H:|\\xi|^2\\mathcal F_2f\\in H\\}.$$\n\nThen $H_0$ is self-adjoint, $D(H_0)$ coincides with the Sobolev space $W^{2,2}(\\mathbb R^d;\\mathbb C)$ as a set (with equivalent norms, [[def-sobolev-space-wkp-and-its-norm]], [[thm-fourier-characterisation-of-integer-order-hilbert-sobolev-spaces]]), $H_0\\ge b$ in the quadratic-form sense, and for every Schwartz function $f\\in\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^d)$, $H_0f=-(c/4\\pi^2)\\Delta f+bf$.\n\n(c) If $V:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\mathbb R$ is measurable with $\\|V\\|_\\infty<\\infty$ and $H:=H_0+M_V$ with domain $D(H_0)$, then $H$ is self-adjoint on $D(H_0)$, is bounded below, and the assignment $t\\mapsto e^{itH}$ is a strongly continuous one-parameter unitary group whose generator is $iH$: the limit $\\lim_{t\\to0}\\frac1t(e^{itH}\\psi-\\psi)$ exists exactly for $\\psi\\in D(H)$ and equals $iH\\psi$ ([[lem-self-adjoint-operator-generates-a-strongly-continuous-unitary-group]]).\n\n(d) *Scope*: the argument uses only boundedness and measurability of $V$; it proves nothing for $V\\propto-1/|x|$, $V\\propto|x|^2$ or the delta interaction, does not identify $H_0$ with any measured free-particle Hamiltonian, and asserts no physical interpretation.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Use only under the exact hypotheses and conventions in the quoted section; the consumer states the specific use in its Facts & Assumptions.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published carrier; complete current Proof section inspected for the actual hypotheses and argument. This is not an independent approval claim.",
          "consumer_use": "Direct dependency declared by thm-nrqmx-rank-one-resonance-continuation; relevant local fact labels: "
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-polar-coordinates-formula-for-lebesgue-measure",
          "carrier": "items/thm-polar-coordinates-formula-for-lebesgue-measure.md",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $n \\ge 1$,\nlet $S^{n-1} \\subseteq \\mathbb R^n$ be the unit sphere, and let $\\sigma$ be the\nset function of [[def-polar-surface-measure-on-the-unit-sphere]]. Then $\\sigma$\nis a finite Borel measure on $S^{n-1}$, and for every Borel measurable\n$f : \\mathbb R^n \\to [0,\\infty]$,\n$$\\int_{\\mathbb R^n} f(x)\\,d\\lambda_n(x) = \\int_0^\\infty \\int_{S^{n-1}} f(r\\omega) r^{n-1}\\,d\\sigma(\\omega)\\,dr.$$\nMoreover, $\\sigma$ is the unique Borel measure on $S^{n-1}$ with this\nproperty.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Use only under the exact hypotheses and conventions in the quoted section; the consumer states the specific use in its Facts & Assumptions.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published carrier; complete current Proof section inspected for the actual hypotheses and argument. This is not an independent approval claim.",
          "consumer_use": "Direct dependency declared by thm-nrqmx-rank-one-resonance-continuation; relevant local fact labels: F5"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "cor-lebesgue-sigma-algebra-is-the-completion-of-borel-lebesgue-measure",
          "carrier": "items/cor-lebesgue-sigma-algebra-is-the-completion-of-borel-lebesgue-measure.md",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $n \\ge 1$ and **assume the Axiom of Countable Choice**\n([[def-countable-choice]]). Write $\\beta_n$ for the restriction of $\\lambda_n$ to\nthe Borel sigma-algebra $\\mathcal{B}(\\mathbb{R}^n)$\n([[def-borel-sigma-algebra]]). Then $\\mathcal{L}(\\mathbb{R}^n)$ is exactly the\ncompletion domain of $(\\mathbb{R}^n, \\mathcal{B}(\\mathbb{R}^n), \\beta_n)$\n([[def-completion-of-a-measure-space]]), and $\\lambda_n$ is the completed measure\nthere. Explicitly,\n\n$$E \\in \\mathcal{L}(\\mathbb{R}^n) \\iff E = A\\cup N \\text{ for some } A, Z \\in \\mathcal{B}(\\mathbb{R}^n) \\text{ with } N \\subseteq Z \\text{ and } \\beta_n(Z) = 0,$$\n\nand then $\\lambda_n(E) = \\beta_n(A)$.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Use only under the exact hypotheses and conventions in the quoted section; the consumer states the specific use in its Facts & Assumptions.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published carrier; complete current Proof section inspected for the actual hypotheses and argument. This is not an independent approval claim.",
          "consumer_use": "Direct dependency declared by thm-nrqmx-rank-one-resonance-continuation; relevant local fact labels: F6"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-completion-measurable-functions-have-base-measurable-representatives",
          "carrier": "items/thm-completion-measurable-functions-have-base-measurable-representatives.md",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. Let $(X,\\mathcal{A}_0,\\mu)$ be a measure space and let\n$(X,\\overline{\\mathcal{A}_0},\\overline{\\mu})$ be its completion. If\n$f : X \\to \\overline{\\mathbb{R}}$ is measurable with respect to\n$\\overline{\\mathcal{A}_0}$, then there is an $\\mathcal{A}_0$-measurable function\n$g : X \\to \\overline{\\mathbb{R}}$ such that $f=g$ almost everywhere.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Use only under the exact hypotheses and conventions in the quoted section; the consumer states the specific use in its Facts & Assumptions.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published carrier; complete current Proof section inspected for the actual hypotheses and argument. This is not an independent approval claim.",
          "consumer_use": "Direct dependency declared by thm-nrqmx-rank-one-resonance-continuation; relevant local fact labels: F6"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-self-adjoint-resolvent-estimate",
          "carrier": "items/thm-self-adjoint-resolvent-estimate.md",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $T$ be a self-adjoint operator on $H$. Then every nonreal number belongs to\n$\\rho(T)$: $\\mathbb C\\setminus\\mathbb R\\subseteq\\rho(T)$. More precisely, for\n$z=a+ib$ with $a,b\\in\\mathbb R$, $b\\ne0$, and every $x\\in D(T)$,\n$$\\|(T-z)x\\|^2=\\|(T-a)x\\|^2+b^2\\|x\\|^2 ,$$\nand consequently $\\|R_T(z)\\|\\le 1/|\\operatorname{Im}z|$. In particular\n$\\sigma(T)\\subseteq\\mathbb R$.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Use only under the exact hypotheses and conventions in the quoted section; the consumer states the specific use in its Facts & Assumptions.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published carrier; complete current Proof section inspected for the actual hypotheses and argument. This is not an independent approval claim.",
          "consumer_use": "Direct dependency declared by thm-nrqmx-rank-one-resonance-continuation; relevant local fact labels: F3"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-nrqmx-rank-one-resolvent",
          "carrier": "items/thm-nrqmx-rank-one-resolvent.md",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $H_0$ be a self-adjoint operator on a complex\nHilbert space $H$, with inner product linear in its first variable and\n$\\inf\\sigma(H_0)>-\\infty$. Let $g\\in H$, $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R$, and define\n$P_g:H\\to H$ by $P_gf=\\langle f,g\\rangle g$. Put\n$H_\\lambda=H_0+\\lambda P_g$ on $D(H_\\lambda)=D(H_0)$ and\n$R_0(z)=(H_0-zI)^{-1}$. Then $H_\\lambda$ is self-adjoint. For every\n$z\\in\\rho(H_0)$ such that\n$d_\\lambda(z):=1+\\lambda\\langle R_0(z)g,g\\rangle\\ne0$, one has\n$z\\in\\rho(H_\\lambda)$ and\n$$R_\\lambda(z):=(H_\\lambda-zI)^{-1}=R_0(z)-\\frac{\\lambda R_0(z)P_gR_0(z)}{1+\\lambda\\langle R_0(z)g,g\\rangle}.$$\nFor real $E<\\inf\\sigma(H_0)$ and $g\\ne0$, $E$ is an eigenvalue of $H_\\lambda$\nif and only if $d_\\lambda(E)=0$; in that case the eigenspace is one-dimensional.",
